  "analysis": "Market read: the last session was broadly risk-off — nearly every asset closed negative (A02 -0.5%, A08 -1.3%, A15 -1.5%, A07 -2.5%) while energy spiked (A03 +2.6%, A13 +2.8%). Over 20 days the rotation is into energy (+11.8%/+14.1%), health (+5.0%/+7.6%) and bonds (+3.0%), and out of tech (A11 -5.4%, A01 -6.3%), emerging (A12 -8.8%) and gold (A05 -6.8%). The portfolio is in a small drawdown (-1.8%) with no forced de-risking actions. Forecast medians carry no directional skill, so I lean on interval width as the reliable risk signal: the narrowest 5-day intervals are A02 (0.040), A09 (0.041), A06 (0.043) and A08 (0.048), while the widest are A14 (0.119), A01 (0.118), A07 (0.107), A13 (0.089), A12 (0.082), A05 (0.077) and A03 (0.073). The current book already reflects this: it is overweight the low-width core (A02, A08, A09, A10, A06), has zeroed the widest-interval single stocks (A01, A07, A14) and emerging (A12), cut gold to 1.9%, and holds 17.2% cash. My one change: trim A03 (energy) from 7.12% to 5.00% — it is the only high-width asset held above baseline, and after a +11.75% 20-day run plus a +2.6% spike on a down day, its wide 5-day interval (q10 -3.6%) argues for cutting tail risk rather than chasing momentum. Proceeds go to cash, lifting it to 19.3%. All group caps are respected (us_broad 20.5%, financials 17.9%, energy 8.1%, all < 30%), no ETF exceeds 25%, no stock exceeds 10%, and cash stays well above the 10% floor. Confidence is kept modest (0.5-0.6) because the median forecast has no skill and my edge is risk-based, not directional.",
